 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE PILGRIMS' WAY. Lindisfarne is an island twice a day. The tide covers the causeway, and the safe crossing times are on the board by the road. Check them. The refuge box on stilts is for people who didn't."
  },
  {
   "x": 64,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "LINDISFARNE PRIORY. Founded by St Aidan in 635. Here the monks made the Lindisfarne Gospels, a book so beautiful it now lives under glass in the British Library. Vikings raided it in 793, the first big raid on Britain."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "LINDISFARNE CASTLE. A tiny fort on a lump of whinstone built in the 1500s, then turned into a holiday home in 1901 by an architect who knew a good view when he saw one."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "BAMBURGH. Seat of the kings of Northumbria, on a crag of old volcanic rock. The first castle in England to fall to gunpowder, in the Wars of the Roses. These days: weddings, school trips and very large armour."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 50,
   "text": "THE BEACH BELOW THE CASTLE. A wide sandy beach under a very large castle. Locals say it is the best beach in England and keep very quiet about it, which is not working."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "THE FARNE ISLANDS. In 1838 Grace Darling, aged twenty-two, and her father rowed out from Longstone Lighthouse through a storm to rescue nine shipwreck survivors. Boats from Seahouses go out to see the puffins."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "ROUTE SOUTH. Left for the long beach and Seahouses. Right for Craster, and kippers. Straight on for the Wall, Newcastle, and an entirely different accent."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "northumberland_coast_cuthbert",
   "title": "Cuddy's Ducks",
   "text": "St Cuthbert, Bishop of Lindisfarne, lived as a hermit on Inner Farne and is said to have protected the eider ducks nesting there, among the first bird protection rules in Britain. Locals still call eiders 'Cuddy's ducks'. Cuthbert died on Inner Farne in 687."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 46,
   "plaque": "northumberland_coast_northumbria",
   "title": "Name: Northumbria",
   "text": "Northumbria means the land of the people north of the Humber. At its height in the 7th century the Anglo-Saxon kingdom stretched from the Humber to the Firth of Forth, ruled from Bamburgh, and the county of Northumberland still carries the name."
  }
 ],
